@startuml ota-confirm
' Why an image flashed by ST-Link has to be confirmed by hand, and the one
' answer that looks like a driver fault and is not. Grounded in
' Src/ota_update.c (on_confirm), Src/ota_port.c (confirm),
' Src/flash.c (flash_write_confirm_record, record_state) and ota-confirm.ps1.

title Keeping an image that ST-Link wrote

skinparam shadowing false

start

:host sends CONFIRM (0x05), no payload;

if (an update session is running?) then (yes)
  :NAK BAD_STATE;
  stop
else (no)
endif

if (the running image is already CONFIRMED?) then (yes)
  :ACK, nothing written;
  note right
    Repeating it is harmless.
    A record already in place is
    left alone, so the script is
    safe to run after every flash.
  end note
  stop
else (no, on trial)
endif

switch (what is in the record page?)
case (erased)
  :write the record
  (magic plus this image's CRC32);
  :ACK;
case (the same record)
  :nothing to do;
  :ACK;
case (a DIFFERENT image's record)
  :NAK FLASH_ERROR;
  note right
    NOT a broken driver. Flash cannot
    be overwritten, only erased, and
    an ST-Link flash never clears this
    page because it sits past the end
    of the image. Answering OK here
    would promise a confirmation that
    does not hold.

    The host erases the one page and
    runs this again.
  end note
endswitch

stop

legend bottom
  An image nobody confirmed is one watchdog reset away from being replaced
  by whatever sits in the other bank. On 2026-11-02 that is exactly what
  happened: the board kept answering buttons while running version 5 from
  bank 2, and an evening of measurements was taken on the wrong firmware.
endlegend

@enduml
